# Architecture

One window, two worlds: the **frontend** (React in the system webview) and the
**core** (Rust). Everything crossing the boundary goes through Tauri's IPC,
and this project wraps that boundary with conventions enforced by `dude lint`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph Webview [React frontend — src/]
        P[pages/] --> I[ipc/ typed wrappers]
        P --> H[hooks/useTauriEvent]
    end
    subgraph Core [Rust core — src-tauri/]
        C[commands/*.rs] --> S[state.rs — AppState]
{{#if withSqlite}}
        C --> D[db.rs — SQLite]
{{/if}}
        C --> E[error.rs — AppError]
    end
    I -- "invoke('cmd', args)" --> C
    C -- "app.emit(event, payload)" --> H
```

## Commands (request/response)

A command is a Rust function annotated `#[tauri::command]`, living in
`src-tauri/src/commands/<domain>.rs` and registered in `generate_handler![…]`
in `lib.rs`. The frontend calls it only through the matching typed wrapper in
`src/ipc/<domain>.ts`:

```ts
// src/ipc/greet.ts
export async function greet(name: string): Promise<string> {
  return invoke<string>('greet', { name })
}
```

Argument keys are camelCase in TS, snake_case in Rust — Tauri converts.
Returned structs serialize with `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]` so the TS
interfaces match verbatim.

## Managed state

`AppState` (`state.rs`) is registered once with `app.manage()` and injected
into commands as `tauri::State<'_, AppState>`. Commands run concurrently, so
mutable fields sit behind a `Mutex`. The backend owns the truth; the frontend
renders it.

## Events (push)

When backend state changes, commands broadcast with
`app.emit(COUNTER_CHANGED_EVENT, value)`. The frontend subscribes through the
`useTauriEvent` hook, which owns the subscribe/unsubscribe lifecycle. The Home
page counter demonstrates the full loop: invoke → state change → event →
re-render.

## Errors

Every fallible command returns `Result<_, AppError>` (`error.rs`). `AppError`
implements `Serialize`, so a rejection reaches the frontend as a clean
message. Add variants (with `#[from]`) per failure domain and propagate with
`?` — never `unwrap()` in a command.

{{#if withSqlite}}
## Persistence (SQLite)

`db.rs` opens `app.db` in the platform app-data directory at startup and
applies append-only migrations. The connection is managed as `Db(Mutex<Connection>)`.
Command modules keep query logic in plain functions over `&Connection`, so the
unit tests run the real schema against an in-memory database.

{{/if}}
## Security: capabilities & CSP

`src-tauri/capabilities/default.json` grants the main window exactly the
permissions it uses; `tauri.conf.json` sets a strict CSP that Tauri augments
at runtime. Widen either only when a feature demands it (rules BE009/BE010).

## The conventions, in one table

| Rule | Enforces |
| ---- | -------- |
| FE001–FE008 | pages/components/hooks/assets layout and barrels |
| FE009–FE011 | IPC only via `src/ipc/`, events only via `useTauriEvent` |
| BE001–BE005 | backend skeleton, thin main.rs, command placement & registration |
| BE006–BE008 | AppError everywhere, no panics in commands, log crate over print |
| BE009–BE011 | config/capability hygiene, unit tests per command module |

Every rule is documented in `.claude/rules/` and checked by `dude lint`.
